MultiPEM Docker test workflow
=============================

This directory provides one versioned Docker runtime for the verification
tests stored in `../Test`. The base-R launcher creates an auditable, writable
job snapshot and starts each test as a detached container by default. Test
output never writes into the canonical `Test`, `Code`, or `Applications`
directories.

Host requirements
-----------------

The host needs:

* R 4.0 or newer, with `Rscript` on `PATH`;
* a Docker CLI connected to a running Docker engine configured for Linux
  containers;
* a processor architecture supported by the pinned base image, or Docker
  emulation for that architecture;
* permission for Docker to bind-mount the repository and results locations;
  and
* enough disk space for the runtime image, job snapshots, and test output.

No host R packages, Bash, Docker Compose, compiler toolchain, or symbolic
links are required. The optional `./testmpem` convenience wrapper needs a
POSIX `/bin/sh`; `Rscript testmpem.R` is the portable interface for macOS,
Linux, and Windows. Building the image or refreshing dependencies requires
network access. Test jobs need no network access after the image is built.

`Test-Docker` must remain beside the source trees it snapshots:

    MultiPEM_Toolbox/
        Applications/
        Code/
        Test/
        Test-Docker/

On macOS and Windows, configure Docker Desktop to share the repository and
results locations with its Linux virtual machine. Native Windows users can
run the `Rscript testmpem.R` commands from PowerShell or Command Prompt. A
remote Docker daemon works only if it can access each bind-mounted path at
exactly the path sent by the client.

Run the diagnostic before the first build and whenever the environment
changes:

    Rscript testmpem.R doctor

It validates the host R version, repository layout, test-group mappings,
application code and fixture locations, Docker connection, Linux-container
mode, and runtime-image availability.

Run the host-side launcher contract checks after changing `testmpem.R` or its
registry handling:

    Rscript test-launcher.R

These checks exercise suite discovery and assert clean rejection of path
traversal, unknown suites and commands, missing option values, invalid CPU and
volume-label settings, incomplete status requests, and malformed registry
schemas. They use disposable temporary fixtures and do not start a numerical
test container.

Configuration
-------------

The launcher accepts these optional environment variables:

* `TEST_MPEM_IMAGE` selects the image name and tag. Its default is
  `mpem/test-runtime:4.4.3`.
* `TEST_MPEM_RESULTS_ROOT` selects the parent directory for automatically
  named jobs. Its default is `Test-Docker/results`.
* `TEST_MPEM_VOLUME_LABEL` may be `z` or `Z` when bind mounts require an
  SELinux relabeling option. Leave it unset on other systems.

Command-line options such as `--results`, `--cpus`, and `--memory` apply to a
single test job.

Build and run
-------------

From `Test-Docker`:

    Rscript testmpem.R build
    Rscript testmpem.R list
    Rscript testmpem.R run global
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Seismic
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Checkpoint
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Bayes

`list` discovers test suites from canonical `tests.r` files under `../Test`.
`global` selects `../Test/tests.r`; a nested name selects the corresponding
directory, such as `../Test/IYDT/Seismic/tests.r`.

Every invocation without `--results` creates a new unique results directory
under `Test-Docker/results`. Its name includes the suite, UTC start time, and
launcher process ID. To put a job elsewhere, supply an empty or nonexistent
directory:

    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Acoustic --results /path/to/acoustic-results

Jobs run in the background unless `--foreground` is supplied. The detached
command prints the container name and results directory. Use that name with:

    Rscript testmpem.R jobs
    Rscript testmpem.R status JOB
    Rscript testmpem.R logs -f JOB
    Rscript testmpem.R wait JOB
    Rscript testmpem.R stop JOB
    Rscript testmpem.R clean JOB

`logs -f` follows live output. `wait` waits for completion and returns the
test exit code. `stop` terminates a running test. `clean` removes an exited
managed container while retaining all result files. Containers remain after
completion until explicitly cleaned, so Docker status and logs remain
available.

An explicit name and resource limits are optional:

    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Crater --name crater-check --cpus 4 --memory 8g

Container names must be unique. Stop or wait for a running container before
cleaning it. Remove a completed container or choose a new name before reusing
an explicit name.

Job snapshots and output
------------------------

Each new job gets a private snapshot. A global job has this shape:

    RESULTS/
        .testmpem/
            created
            image-id
            initial-files.sha256
            last-container
            test
            worker-controls.tsv
        work/
            Test/
                Code/
                tests.r

A nested group also receives its registered application code and fixtures:

    RESULTS/work/Test/IYDT/
        Code/
        Test/
        Seismic/tests.r

For a `Runfile`, `Bayes`, `Bayes-Backends`, `MultiPhenomenology`, `Parallel`,
`Prediction`, `Profile`, or `Imputation`
suite, the snapshot also contains only the registered smoke case plus the
shared code, IYDT code, crater prior code, and data needed by that case.
`Runfile` reduces the copied deck to one MLE start, one worker, one event
imputation, and no Bayesian phase. `Bayes` instead uses fixed seeds, one
worker, short burn-in and sampling chains, and numerical derivative
assertions. `Bayes-Backends` executes bounded real-application RAM and NUTS
chains. `Parallel` compares deterministic one-worker and two-worker workflows.
`Prediction` directly validates fitted/observed and covariance output from
`predict.r`; `Profile` runs a reduced five-point profile-likelihood workflow.
`Imputation` runs two calibration-draw event analyses through RAM and SMC and
checks pooled posterior dimensions and per-imputation diagnostics.
The `MultiPhenomenology` suite additionally stages the optical and crater
single-phenomenology cases, then uses their generated optimization files in a
combined workflow. In every case only the private snapshot is edited; the
repository runfile is not changed.

The test executes from its suite directory in the snapshot. Canonical source
files are copied with their relative layout intact, then worker-pool calls in
the snapshot are guarded against CPU oversubscription. Initial SHA-256 hashes
record the prepared inputs. Later source edits cannot change a running or
previously prepared job.

The selected suite directory contains:

    .RData
    .testmpem.status
    test-job.out
    tests.out
    tests.cpu-adjustments.log

`.RData` contains objects produced by the canonical `tests.r`. `tests.out` and
the CPU log describe the latest invocation. `test-job.out` appends console
output across every invocation of the same job directory. The status file
records the current state, timestamps, suite, and exit code. Docker retains
the same console stream while the managed container exists.

Reusing `--results` deliberately reruns the same preserved snapshot. The
launcher rejects a directory assigned to another suite, a different runtime
image, an incomplete snapshot, or an unrecognized nonempty directory. Use a
new results directory when the job should include newer canonical source.

CPU and memory assignment
-------------------------

The runtime detects CPU capacity visible to the container. Before a snapshot
is run, calls that pass the MultiPEM variables `ncor`, `ncor_map`, `ncor_mc`,
`ncor_smc`, `ncores_mle`, `ncores_map`, `ncores_mc`, or `ncores_smc` as a
worker-pool size are instrumented. If a requested pool exceeds available
capacity, only the effective pool is reduced. The logical `ncores*` variable
is left unchanged, preserving calculation branches, batching, seeds, and
object contents that depend on it. The test continues and writes a warning
with the requested and assigned counts to both console output and
`tests.cpu-adjustments.log`.

Use `--cpus N` to impose a smaller Docker CPU quota. The worker ceiling is
`max(1, floor(N))`; fractional quota is still honored by Docker. Without this
option, Docker's visible CPU/cgroup capacity is the ceiling. `--memory SIZE`
passes a Docker memory limit such as `8g`. A memory limit can cause the
container to fail if a test needs more memory; it does not change numerical
methods.

The instrumentation report in `.testmpem/worker-controls.tsv` identifies each
guarded file, line, variable, and control type. CPU limiting changes
concurrency, not mathematical inputs. Floating-point operation order in
parallel libraries can still produce ordinary numerical roundoff differences.

Running all supplied tests
--------------------------

The supplied suites are independent jobs. Run each one and wait for its
printed job name:

    Rscript testmpem.R run global
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Seismic
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Acoustic
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Crater
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Optical
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Data
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Transform
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Prior
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Checkpoint
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Contract
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Summary
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Runfile
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Bayes
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Bayes-Backends
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/MultiPhenomenology
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Parallel
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Prediction
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Profile
    Rscript testmpem.R run IYDT/Imputation

Starting several jobs simultaneously divides neither CPUs nor memory between
them. To prevent aggregate oversubscription, run them sequentially or assign
`--cpus` limits whose total does not exceed host capacity.

IYDT suites assert output dimensions, finite values, and numerical tolerances.
A derivative mismatch therefore gives the job a nonzero exit status;
reviewing a printed difference range is not sufficient.

Adding tests
------------

To add a suite to an existing registered group, create its canonical runner:

    Test/IYDT/NewPhenomenology/tests.r

Use the same relative layout as the other IYDT runners: group application code
is available at `../Code`, and group fixtures at `../Test`. `testmpem.R list`
discovers the suite automatically; no Dockerfile or image rebuild is required.
A new job snapshots the new source.

To add an entirely new test group:

1. Put each canonical runner under `Test/GROUP/SUITE/tests.r`.
2. Put application code under `Applications/Code/CODE_DIRECTORY`.
3. Put shared test fixtures under `Applications/Test/FIXTURE_DIRECTORY`.
4. Add exactly one tab-separated row to `test-groups.tsv`. Use `-` when no
   secondary code tree is needed; the last three fields select the runfile
   family, relative smoke-case directory, and application data:

       tests  code  fixtures  secondary_code  smoke_runfiles  smoke_case  smoke_data
       GROUP  CODE  FIXTURES  -               RUNFILES        PATH        DATA

5. Run `Rscript testmpem.R doctor`, then `Rscript testmpem.R list` and the new
   suite.

The `tests` value must equal the top-level directory below `Test`. The `code`
and `fixtures` values name directories below `Applications/Code` and
`Applications/Test`. In the snapshot these are placed at
`Test/GROUP/Code` and `Test/GROUP/Test`, respectively. The smoke fields
identify paths below `Runfiles` and `Applications/Data` and are copied only
for a runfile integration suite. The currently recognized integration suite
names are `Runfile`, `Bayes`, `Bayes-Backends`, `MultiPhenomenology`,
`Parallel`, `Prediction`, `Profile`, and `Imputation`.
`MultiPhenomenology` staging also copies its optical and
two-phenomenology runfile cases.

To add another application-independent test, edit `Test/tests.r` or files it
sources from `Code`. The `global` job always snapshots the complete `Code`
directory as `Test/Code`.

Dependencies and reproducibility
--------------------------------

`Dockerfile.runtime` pins the base image by digest and `renv.lock` pins R
packages. Test source is mounted from an immutable-per-job snapshot, so source
changes do not require an image rebuild. Rebuild only when the runtime or lock
changes:

    Rscript testmpem.R refresh-lock
    Rscript testmpem.R build --no-cache

Refreshing dependencies is a maintainer operation and requires network
access. Review lock changes and rerun every suite after a refresh.

Compare two saved workspaces when validating a calculation change:

    Rscript testmpem.R compare left.RData right.RData
    Rscript testmpem.R compare left.RData right.RData 1e-10

The comparison requires identical object names and uses R's `all.equal` at
the requested tolerance for each object.

Saved test workspaces are disposable artifacts. In production benchmarking
and calibration workflows, preserve a pristine `.RData` checkpoint before an
event analysis and run each event from a copy of that checkpoint.

Troubleshooting
---------------

If `doctor` cannot reach Docker, start the engine and verify the current user
can run `docker info`. If a bind mount is denied, share the path in Docker
Desktop or set `TEST_MPEM_VOLUME_LABEL=z` on an SELinux host. If a detached
job fails, inspect `status`, `logs`, the suite's `tests.out`, and
`.testmpem.status`. All source and generated artifacts remain in the printed
results directory even after `clean` removes the container.
